# Project guidance documentation (always apply)

## Where docs live

- At each **standalone / deployable project root** (e.g. `gdtracker/`, `gdtracker-api/`), maintain guidance the team and agents use when changing code or planning features.
- A **workspace-level** index is optional; the rule applies per app root unless you explicitly scope a different layout in chat.

## Shape: one file or a folder

- **Small projects**: a single `PROJECT.md` at the project root, or **`docs/README.md`** only, is enough.
- **Growing projects**: prefer **`docs/`** with an index (`docs/README.md`) that links focused files, for example `docs/OVERVIEW.md`, `docs/FEATURES.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/data.md`—split when sections get long or domains diverge.

## What to include

- **Purpose**: one short paragraph—what the app does for users or operators.
- **Directory map**: where routes/pages, API clients, shared UI, controllers, services, repositories, entities, migrations, and tests live.
- **Feature map**: each major feature or screen → primary source files (so changes start in the right place).
- **Stack and decisions**: framework, routing, state/data approach, build tool, runtime version, **database** (engine and how schema is managed), auth/deployment notes if relevant—state *why* only when it affects future work.
- **Run / develop**: minimal pointers (`npm run dev`, `./mvnw spring-boot:run`) or link to an existing README; avoid duplicating long install guides.

## Maintenance (required with feature work)

Treat project guidance as part of the deliverable whenever you touch product behavior.

- **New features**: Before finishing the task, **update that project’s docs**—at minimum the **feature map** (`docs/FEATURES.md` or equivalent), and any new routes, API modules, controllers, entities, or migrations that the map should reference. If the change alters stack or layout, update **`docs/OVERVIEW.md`** / **`docs/README.md`** (or `PROJECT.md`) too.
- **Feature changes or updates**: Same rule—if behavior, endpoints, screens, data model, or file locations shift, **edit the docs in the same change** so the feature map and overview stay accurate. Do not leave docs describing the old shape.
- **Structural / persistence changes** (new packages, services layer, DB strategy, major refactors): update the **directory map** and **stack/decisions** sections even when the change is not user-visible.

When planning or answering “where does X live?”, **read that project’s guidance docs first** before scanning the whole tree; after your change, those docs must still be truthful.

## Security

- **Never** copy **passwords, API keys, tokens, or full JDBC URLs with credentials** from `application.properties`, `.env`, or secrets stores into project docs. Describe configuration in generic terms (e.g. “PostgreSQL; Spring `spring.datasource.*` / environment variables”).
